---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import RangeControl from "../components/RangeControl.astro";
import StageSection from "../components/StageSection.astro";
import ToggleControl from "../components/ToggleControl.astro";
---

<Layout title="How a rasterized render pipeline works — COMP4020 prototype">
  <header class="site-header">
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="#model">Model</a>
      <a href="#world">World</a>
      <a href="#view">View</a>
      <a href="#projection">Projection</a>
      <a href="#rasterize">Rasterize</a>
      <a href="#shading">Shading</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>How a rasterized render pipeline works</h1>
      <p>
        Every triangle drawn in real time — in a game, a CAD tool, a browser's
        canvas — passes through the same six stages. Scroll down to walk
        through each one on a live low-poly sphere, tweaking the numbers as
        you go. The last stage is the one that matters most: watch the exact
        same triangles get lit two different ways.
      </p>
    </section>

    <StageSection id="model" number={1} title="Model space">
      <p>
        Here's the raw geometry: a set of vertices and the triangles that
        connect them, defined once and untouched by any transform, camera, or
        light. This icosphere starts as a 12-vertex icosahedron; each
        subdivision splits every triangle into four and pushes the new points
        back onto the sphere.
      </p>
      <RangeControl id="model-subdivisions" label="Subdivisions" min={0} max={3} step={1} value={1} />
    </StageSection>

    <StageSection id="world" number={2} title="Model → world transform">
      <p>
        A model's vertices are defined around its own origin. Placing it in a
        scene means multiplying every vertex by a transform matrix — here, a
        rotation and a uniform scale — to get its position in shared world
        space.
      </p>
      <RangeControl id="world-rotation" label="Rotation" min={0} max={360} step={1} value={45} unit="°" />
      <RangeControl id="world-scale" label="Scale" min={0.5} max={2} step={0.05} value={1} />
    </StageSection>

    <StageSection id="view" number={3} title="World → view (camera) transform">
      <p>
        The camera has its own position and orientation in world space. The
        view transform re-expresses every vertex relative to the camera
        instead — as if the camera were nailed to the origin, looking down
        its own axis.
      </p>
      <RangeControl id="view-azimuth" label="Camera azimuth" min={-180} max={180} step={1} value={40} unit="°" />
      <RangeControl id="view-elevation" label="Camera elevation" min={-80} max={80} step={1} value={20} unit="°" />
      <RangeControl id="view-distance" label="Camera distance" min={1.8} max={6} step={0.1} value={3.2} />
    </StageSection>

    <StageSection id="projection" number={4} title="Projection & clip">
      <p>
        The projection matrix maps view-space points into clip space; dividing
        by the resulting <em>w</em> gives normalized device coordinates in
        [-1, 1] on every axis — the perspective divide that makes distant
        things smaller.
      </p>
      <RangeControl id="projection-fov" label="Field of view" min={20} max={120} step={1} value={55} unit="°" />
      <RangeControl id="projection-distance" label="Camera distance" min={1.8} max={6} step={0.1} value={3.2} />
    </StageSection>

    <StageSection id="rasterize" number={5} title="Rasterization">
      <p>
        Rasterizing a triangle means deciding which pixels its on-screen
        corners cover, using an edge function to test each pixel and a
        z-buffer to keep only the nearest triangle at every pixel. Coarser
        meshes make the underlying flat triangles obvious; finer ones
        approach a smooth curve.
      </p>
      <RangeControl
        id="rasterize-subdivisions"
        label="Mesh detail (subdivisions)"
        min={0}
        max={3}
        step={1}
        value={1}
      />
    </StageSection>

    <StageSection id="shading" number={6} title="Shading: per-vertex vs per-pixel">
      <p>
        The rasterizer above fills every triangle identically either way —
        what changes is <em>when</em> lighting is computed.
        <strong>Gouraud</strong> shading lights each vertex once and interpolates
        the resulting color across the triangle. <strong>Phong</strong> shading
        interpolates the surface normal instead, and lights every pixel
        individually. On a curved, low-poly surface like this one, Gouraud's
        flat-colored triangles show up as visible banding; Phong keeps
        highlights smooth.
      </p>
      <ToggleControl
        id="shading-mode-toggle"
        label="Shading mode"
        onLabel="Per-pixel (Phong)"
        offLabel="Per-vertex (Gouraud)"
      />
      <RangeControl id="shading-light-azimuth" label="Light azimuth" min={-180} max={180} step={1} value={40} unit="°" />
      <RangeControl id="shading-shininess" label="Shininess" min={2} max={128} step={1} value={28} />
    </StageSection>
  </main>
  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
